Z||000000||M King||02-12-2000||04:22 PM||||I have a few questions on fieros, What is the road and wind noise like inside fieros at normal highway speeds, better or worse then most cars today?? And also What this diff is between the 86 and 86.5 GT, Was one fastback and one notchback? Thanks!||||reg||5
Z||000001||ggarsk||02-12-2000||04:36 PM||||I'd say the road noise is about average. I know I have rode in cars that are a lot worse. If you are thinking about buying a Fiero,go for it! You won't regret it and if you have any problems they can usually be solved here in the forum.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Patrick||02-12-2000||04:57 PM||||<BR>The only "noise" I notice with my '87 that might be a little louder than with other cars is the sound of the engine. Of course, that's because the engine is only a very short distance behind your head! However, I don't mind it at all. I actually enjoy listening to it purring away. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000003||GT Bastard||02-12-2000||05:45 PM||||INTERIOR SOUND LEVEL<P>idle...........................................51dBA<BR>Full Throttle acceleration (the best kind).....78dBA<BR>70mph crusing..................................76dBA<BR>70mph coasting.................................73dBA<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||grizco||02-14-2000||12:24 AM||||The road noise in my fiero is a lot quieter than my S10. When I crank the radio up you can't here any road noise.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Old Lar||02-14-2000||12:17 PM||||86.5 Fiero were made after June 86. Pontiac started installing the Getrag 5 speed manual transmission, replacing the 4 speed manual transmission.  Essentially it was an 87 Fiero.<P>If you turn the stereo up loud enough, you don't hear any road noise.  Otherwise you hear the sweet rumble of the Fiero exhaust.||||reg||1
Z||000006||william_thorniii||02-14-2000||12:37 PM||||The 86.5 Fiero is usually only talked about in the GT trim.  This is because the 86 GT was a late release.  In the early part of 86 a current model year GT was not available.  This is another one of the Fiero's "urban legends".  Therefore if you come across an 86 GT, it will have the flying buttress style or more commonly known as "fastback" rear pillars.  A transmission was changed even after the release of the 86 GT.  This is where most people get confused since the first 3/4 of 86 Fiero's had the 4-spd or Muncie transmission and the 5-spd or Getrag transmission became available later in the production run.  <P>Is it just me, or does it sound like Pontiac took a vacation after the 85 GT was released?  They DEFINITELY didn't get their homework in on time pertaining to the 86 GT.<P>If anyone has any facts to oppose what I have said please speak up.  The comments that I have expressed have reflected those in the Fiero Spotter's Guide by Mark Corbin.  This is a highly debated topic and I really don't want to get a flame war started here.<P>Thanks,<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000007||Standard||02-15-2000||01:44 AM||||Now I know these numbers are hard to get, but does anybody have a rough guesstimate as to how many GT 5 speeds were made?  I've heard 2000, which would make them one of the rarer models.  I hope so!||||reg||1
Z||000008||ChadMan||02-15-2000||08:44 AM||||I think there were fewer than 2000 1986 GT 5-speeds made, if I'm remembering my "Fiero Spotter's Guide" right.  <P>The first time I saw one, I wouldn't believe it was an 86 because it had the 5-speed.  The owner was really cool and pulled out his registration to prove to me it wasn't an 87 GT.  Boy, was my face red.||||reg||1
